import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from par_helpers import *
save(20,"Meaning, Service and Your Family Toolkit","What is the one sentence I hope my children carry into the world?",[
mc("U","According to Lesson 20, what is the achievement of a life that a person takes with them?",
 ["The reputation and the work that a person leaves behind them for others.","The wealth and security that have been built for the family through the years.","The skills and knowledge gathered in a career, as passed on to the young.","The inner growth, the unfoldment of knowledge, peace, goodness and gratitude."],3,
 "By achievement the lesson means the inner kind: growth, the subjective unfoldment, which is what a person takes from this life. External, material achievements will not follow us beyond it. A family can recognise together what it means to grow: more evolved in knowledge, inner beauty, inner peace, self-worth, freedom, purity, goodness and gratitude.",
 "Inner growth is the destination","Part 1"),
mc("A","A seventeen-year-old asks her mother in the car, “What do you live for? What’s the point?” and her mother has no ready answer. Which response fits Lesson 20?",
 ["Offer what she honestly sees, say “I don’t know” where that is true, and let her test it.","Say “you”, and leave the question there, since that is the true answer.","Give her the family’s tradition clearly, so that she has a firm answer to follow.","Tell her that the question will be answered by itself as she gets older."],0,
 "A parent offers what they see, honestly, and the young person tests it against her own life. It is fine to say “I do not know”: clarity grows along the way, and some clarity at the start is enough. The questions stir in the teenage years and deserve a warm answer, not a silence. The goal is owned by the young person who walks the journey.",
 "Offer what you see, honestly; it is fine to say “I don’t know”","Part 1"),
mc("U","How does the lesson relate a young person’s near goals, such as finishing a degree, to the final destination?",
 ["The near goals are small beside the final goal, and should be held lightly.","The final goal comes first, and the near goals are chosen only to serve it.","The near stations are real, and the final destination makes them meaningful.","The near goals and the final goal are separate matters, and need not meet."],2,
 "A degree finished with care and a friendship kept with honesty are exactly the exercises by which the inner qualities grow. The final destination does not make the near stations small; it makes them meaningful. The young person owns the goal, and their own may not be the ultimate goal, but it is at least a station on the way.",
 "The near stations are real; the destination gives them meaning","Part 1"),
mc("A","A seventeen-year-old is anxious and in tears at midnight. Which response fits Lesson 20?",
 ["Remind her that the world is a gymnasium, and that this is an exercise in strength.","Hear her, hold her and help her now; ask later what it brought out in her.","Tell her to look at how others are worse off, so that she sees her trouble in proportion.","Let her work it through alone, since each person must lift their own weight."],1,
 "Seeing a challenge as an exercise is a lens a young person grows into, never a slogan to hand them in the middle of their pain. In the moment they need to be heard, held and helped. Later, when the storm has eased, a parent can ask together: what did this bring out in you? If it is more than she can lift, bring in family, friends, teachers or professionals.",
 "Heard, held and helped first; the lens comes later","Part 2"),
judge("“If every challenge is an exercise, then the hard things that happen to us are really good things.”",F,
 "No one goes to a gymnasium hoping the weights will be light, and some things are simply bad; no one needs to call them good. The lens is not about the thing itself, but about what meeting it can draw out of us. The question changes from “why is this happening to me?” to “what is this drawing out of me?”, slowly, and never on the first night.",
 "The lens is about what meeting it draws out","Part 2"),
mc("U","How does Lesson 20 describe sacrifice?",
 ["A hardship accepted for the sake of a larger duty, to be borne in silence.","A giving up of one’s own interests that must always be made without complaint.","A positive giving of time and energy for those we love, not harm or self-erasure.","A sign of a deep inner life, which only a few people are called to make."],2,
 "Sacrifice is a positive thing, not harm and not self-erasure. Everyone sacrifices their own time, energy and interests for the greater good of those they love. Parents do it every day for their children. A young person who has watched this at home already knows what it looks like, and in time chooses their own sacrifices freely and gladly.",
 "Sacrifice is a positive giving, not self-erasure","Part 2"),
mc("A","A young person says, “I can’t change the world, so why bother?” Which response fits Lesson 20?",
 ["Each serves by capacity and circumstance; the spirit matters more than the size.","Remind him that great changes begin with a single person, so even he may change the world.","Suggest he find a cause large enough to feel it is worth his effort, and give himself to that.","Tell him that service is a duty, and that a duty does not depend on its results."],0,
 "Each person serves according to their capacity, their circumstances and their disposition. A student may tutor a younger child, a young worker may give a weekend to a cause, and a busy parent may simply give full attention at the dinner table. The size of the act matters less than the spirit in which it is offered. In helping the world we really help ourselves.",
 "The spirit matters more than the size of the act","Part 2"),
mc("A","A father feels that his own voice on life’s meaning is “empty”, since he never asked himself the question. Which approach fits Lesson 20?",
 ["Pass the question to a teacher or a tradition, since they are better placed to answer it.","Put four questions to himself, then share honestly, even “I am still working this out”.","Wait until his children ask, and then give the best answer he can find at the time.","Read widely on the subject first, so that his answer is clear before he gives it."],1,
 "Not empty, but unasked. Four questions a parent can put to themselves: what has given my life meaning, what have I learned from the traditions I grew up with, what carried me through the hardest times, and what do I now know matters most? Some parents have never asked; a child’s questions become an invitation to reflect, and honest sharing teaches more than a polished answer.",
 "Not empty, unasked; share honestly","Part 3"),
judge("“A young person who questions and doubts the family’s values is rejecting them.”",F,
 "Questions and doubts belong in this conversation, not outside it. A young person who questions is not rejecting the family’s values; they are making them their own. The traditions are full of such questioners. A question is the start of the answer, not the end of it.",
 "Questions are the start of the answer","Part 3"),
mc("A","In a mixed family, one parent holds a faith and the other holds none, and their daughter is asking about life’s meaning. Which approach fits Lesson 20?",
 ["Choose one view for the family to hold, so that the daughter is not confused.","Keep the subject out of the home until the parents agree among themselves.","Let each parent share what they hold dear, respect the other’s, and leave her free to seek.","Present the beliefs of both as equal, and ask her to choose the one that appeals to her."],2,
 "The same principle holds. Parents can share what they hold dear, respect what the other holds, and leave the young person free to seek. The destination, a person more evolved in knowledge, inner beauty, inner peace, self-worth and self-dignity, freedom, purity, goodness and gratitude, is one that every tradition can recognise, and so can many people with no tradition at all.",
 "Share, respect, and leave the young person free to seek","Part 3")])
